Hello everyone, my name is Kilic and I'm joining here today from the department of Biochemistry in Tel Aviv University under the supervision of Professor Nir Ben-Tal.

Today I will be presenting on our findings from our preprint "Proteins with multiple GPCR (G-protein coupled receptor) domains".

Our research is on GPCRs, 7-transmembrane helix receptors that transduce extracellular signals to inside the cells. 

Here, we can see a canonical GPCR signal transduction pathway with its structure on the side.

Interesting research is being done by various groups on the functionality and evolution of GPCRs, and some of it is based on how and why they oligomerize.
We have known for some time that GPCRs form homo- and hetero-oligomers.
An example here, we see the structure of the micro-opioid receptor. This GPCR, like many others, forms dimers for its proper activity.

This connects us to our main hypothesis: by some mechanism of evolution could there be proteins with multiple GPCR domains? 
These would be proteins that get translated as a single chain, and fold into the oligomeric structure of multiple seperate GPCRs in vivo, but only by using one chain.

With this question in mind, we devised a search protocol to search for multi-domain GPCRs. In the end, although we didn't do an all inclusive search, we managed to find 57 representative multi-domain GPCRs that are similar to different classes of GPCRs.
Here we can see a schematic representation of a multi-GPCR of two domains on the left-hand side. They are non-overlapping. And each domain has 7 transmembrane helices.
The dotted line here (slide 3) depicts that the linker between two GPCR domains can be variable. 
On the right is an alphafold predicted structure of a candidate multi-domain GPCR we discovered with our pipeline, and it is colored according to the schematic on the left. 

